\section*{Executive Summary}

The Aether-Core/200LY engine is a multi-physics astrophysical simulation
framework designed to model stellar kinematics, plasma dynamics, and
spectral field behavior within a 200-light-year volume centered on the
solar position. The system integrates N-body gravitation, ideal
magnetohydrodynamics (MHD), surrogate Physics-Informed Neural Operator
(PINO) inference, and distributed multi-GPU computation using CUDA and
GPUDirect MPI.

The simulation ingests observational datasets (Gaia DR3, TESS) and
constructs high-resolution spatial fields for density, velocity, and
magnetic structure. Numerical solvers are validated through strict
performance and stability metrics, including energy conservation
$\Delta E / E < 10^{-6}$, surrogate error $\text{NRMSE} < 0.05$, and
communication overhead $T_{\text{comm}} / T_{\text{total}} < 0.12$.
Soft real-time constraints require each physics step to complete within
$t_{\text{step}} \le 0.15 \text{ s}$.

A persistent spectral anomaly at $E = 3.55 \text{ keV}$ was detected and
localized at coordinates:
\[
(120, -45, 80) \text{ LY},
\]
with surrogate inference confidence:
\[
C = 95.8\%.
\]
The anomaly remains stable under chaos injection events, including GPU
memory pressure spikes and MPI network jitter, indicating robustness
against numerical artifacts and transient hardware perturbations.

This executive summary provides a high-level overview of the system
architecture, validation methodology, and anomaly-detection results.
Detailed mathematical formulations, stress-testing protocols, and
reproducibility instructions are provided in the accompanying sections.